We do respect that others have different points of view; we don't respect
all points of view as equally valid. Obviously no one is going to say that
whoever kills the most kobolds should win the tournament, and some people
here think that high in-game score has a similar level of absurdity.
(Actually, since you can only kill 255 kobolds per game, and thus the
"Kobold Killer" challenge would span games, it might actually be _more_
interesting...)

> I'm in favour of variety. If we can somehow track the taming of pets in
> a game, then a trophy for most types of pets would be great! Or even
> one for keeping your original pet alive and tame in its natural form
> through the entire game. These achievements would involve very
> different skillsets than the trophies which are being considered
> meaningful here, but that doesn't lessen them, in my eyes.

I think that keeping your original pet alive (and bringing it to the
Castle, the Sanctum, &c.) would be very difficult indeed! There are many
interesting challenges that could be worth clan or individual tournament
points and would reward different kinds of skill.

> I think the goal that has been expressed by Rast and others is a more
> precise or comprehensive measure of ascension skill. By all means, I'm
> all for this, and rewarding each ascension to encourage people to try
> even if they have no chance at the top trophies is a great idea. But I
> don't see why this process necessitates reducing the variety recognized
> by the tournament. You can create overall "Best" Ascender or "Best
> Ascendingest" Clan recognition and just use those measures appropriate
> to that classification, without affecting the other categories.

I don't think that we want to reduce the variety. I think that we
want to change the variety by removing or reducing the focus on high
in-game score.  I do not think that high in-game score is equally valid
as a gauge of nethack skill, or really any kind of skill. Anyone who
can start a pudding farm can get two billion points by writing some
Perlthonby. (Though as recent games have shown, they can't necessarily
ascend that game...) I for one think that the tournament scoring system
would be better without the in-game high score trophies.

Encouraging people to go for high score also encourages them to spend a 
lot of time; a 2B point game takes longer to play than a 2M point game. 
This encourages players to spend time on repetitive actions in order to 
score tournament points, rather than encourage players to reach out and
try to go for some more challenging achievements. Ascending twice is
worth more than a role high score trophy, and rightly so; honestly I
don't know that the trophies are so bad, as they seem mostly lost in 
the noise of the multiple ascension trophies, but I would rather less 
skilled players were going for more interesting trophies than that. 

As a counterargument, I know that when I and my nethack friends
were lower-skilled players, we fought bitterly over high scores
on our shared server and had a lot of fun doing so. However,
in the tournament, the eit_brads of the world (or really whoever
feels like it) can waltz up, score two billion, and rain on your
Oh-my-God-I-scored-twelve-million-points parade. And that helps neither
the score competitors, whose goal is to get better at the game and
score some points, nor the strongest players, who would rather be doing
something more interesting.

I hope this helps explain why many of us disagree with you.

